  "refuter_findings": [
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:1:1",
      "id": "def-half-open-interval-algebra-on-r",
      "location": "Definition",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The item defines only finite disjoint unions of bounded intervals `E = \\bigcup_{i=1}^m (a_i,b_i]` with real endpoints, then concludes `it is an algebra of subsets of \\mathbb{R}`. But published `def-algebra-of-subsets` requires complement closure and notes `X` belongs to every algebra. Here `\\mathbb{R}` itself is not a finite union of bounded `(a_i,b_i]`, and for `E=(0,1]` the complement `\\mathbb{R}\\setminus E = (-\\infty,0] \\cup (1,\\infty)` is not such a finite union either. So the displayed family is not an algebra of subsets of `\\mathbb{R}`.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "ca830ec2476537b510e5c79b739860754639a5edbf6b606e883fc29e90be63f4"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:1:2",
      "id": "thm-stieltjes-interval-set-function-is-a-premeasure",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The Statement says `\\mu_{0,F}` is `a premeasure on the half-open interval algebra in the sense of [[def-premeasure-on-an-algebra]]`. But published `def-premeasure-on-an-algebra` begins `Let \\mathcal A_0 be an algebra of subsets of X. A premeasure on an algebra ...`. Since scoped `def-half-open-interval-algebra-on-r` does not define an algebra, this item cannot satisfy the cited definition as written.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "a0c7fecfdca6b100ea817164f817134d86a9756a8ad97f522dd7b12444a94339"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:1:3",
      "id": "thm-existence-of-the-lebesgue-stieltjes-measure",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The Statement asserts `Let F : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R} be increasing and right-continuous. Then there is a Borel measure \\mu_F ...`. But its own facts use `[L2] Assuming countable choice, a premeasure extends to a measure on the sigma-algebra it generates`, and step 1.1 invokes `[L2]` to produce the measure `\\mu`. The current proof only yields the existence claim under countable choice, which is absent from the Statement.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "06b1f3e7391348cad3f97a5cfac448b07042aa8f193682f5a497b0b09f5afb68"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:1:4",
      "id": "cex-right-continuity-is-necessary-for-lebesgue-stieltjes-measures",
      "location": "Facts & Assumptions",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The item introduces `Given: The increasing function` with `F(x)=0` for `x\\le 0` and `F(x)=1` for `x>0`. But published `def-monotone-function` defines `increasing` as strict: `f(x) < f(y)` whenever `x < y`. The displayed `F` is constant on `(-\\infty,0]` and on `(0,\\infty)`, so it is not increasing in the cited library sense. The witness therefore does not satisfy the antecedent being refuted.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "eaaeb3967a1e1715f9a2bc988dbccd6f7561b2a398282b0363be9ec82a771e31"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:1:5",
      "id": "ex-cantor-function-is-borel-measurable",
      "location": "2.1",
      "defect": "citation-inaccurate",
      "evidence": "Step 2.1 says `[L2] gives that c is Borel measurable`. But the cited `cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable` states only `Every continuous map f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^m is Borel measurable`, with Given `f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^m`. Here the example's function is `c : [0,1] \\to \\mathbb{R}`. The cited corollary does not cover a subspace domain `[0,1]`, so it does not license step 2.1.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "2d98f8094cea796f87cc30fdd81fe619ae8921cee890d2f96cfcda52a5a423c6"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:1:6",
      "id": "thm-lebesgue-stieltjes-correspondence-with-distribution-functions",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "Clause 1 states `F_\\mu is increasing and right-continuous`. In the current library, `def-monotone-function` makes `increasing` strict. Take the Borel measure `\\delta_0` from published `def-dirac-measure`. The scoped definition `F_\\mu(x)=\\mu((0,x])` for `x\\ge 0` and `F_\\mu(x)=-\\mu((x,0])` for `x<0` gives `F_{\\delta_0}(x)=-1` for `x<0` and `F_{\\delta_0}(x)=0` for `x\\ge 0`, which is right-continuous but not strictly increasing. The proof's own step 1.1 also proves only `F_\\mu(x) \\le F_\\mu(y)`, not strict inequality.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "94d303fb1b84d3338147258a8c6a37363ad893de1c9cab48745ca7f920c419a1"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:1:7",
      "id": "thm-lebesgue-stieltjes-measures-are-regular-on-r",
      "location": "1.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 chooses `t<b` with `F(b^-) - F(t) < \\varepsilon/2` and then concludes `\\mu_F(I \\setminus K) \\le \\mu_F((a,a+\\delta]) + \\mu_F((t,b]) < \\varepsilon` for `K=[a+\\delta,t]`. But `[L1]` gives `\\mu_F((t,b)) = F(b^-) - F(t)` and `\\mu_F((t,b]) = F(b) - F(t)`; the jump `F(b)-F(b^-) = \\mu_F(\\{b\\})` is uncontrolled. For the strictly increasing right-continuous `F(x)=x+\\mathbf 1_{[0,\\infty)}(x)` and `I=(-1,0]`, every such `K` misses `0`, so `\\mu_F(I\\setminus K) \\ge \\mu_F(\\{0\\}) = 1`. The displayed `< \\varepsilon` estimate fails for any `\\varepsilon<1`.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "f186b244857fe1afa172321e057fe51699e3a9db6118ae8e7b287c314742181c"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:1:8",
      "id": "thm-lebesgue-stieltjes-measures-are-regular-on-r",
      "location": "2.1",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "Step 2.1 says `Those traces form an algebra on I_m`, where the traces are sets of the form `(a,b] \\cap I_m`. They do not. For `I_m=[-2,2]`, both `(-2,-1] = (-2,-1] \\cap I_m` and `(0,1] = (0,1] \\cap I_m` are of the stated form, but their union `(-2,-1] \\cup (0,1]` is not equal to `(a,b] \\cap I_m` for any real `a<b`. So the family of single traced half-open intervals is not closed under finite unions and is not an algebra.",
